      "evidence": "The traction claims ($8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth over the last 4 months) are specific, verifiable, and backed by concrete numbers — no hype there. The hype risk is the \"AI-native\" label on slide 1, which is never substantiated anywhere in the deck. The deck inventory records ai_company_status as \"ai_claimed_unverified\": AI claimed but no core AI evidence found (no ML in value prop, no inference/training in COGS, no foundation-model mentions). An investor who notices the AI label and then finds no AI evidence will discount the deck's overall credibility.",
      "notes": "Back the \"AI-native\" claim with a one-sentence description of what the AI component does and why it matters, or remove the label. Every claim in a deck should be supportable in the first five minutes of a meeting."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_features_over_outcomes",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 3 (the solution slide) reads: \"Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI.\" This describes what the product does (wraps four tools) but states no user outcome (time saved, errors eliminated, revenue unlocked). The common-mistake standard requires focusing on value delivered and user outcomes, not technical architecture or feature lists. The slide names four inputs and one output (a CLI) but never states what happens to the user as a result.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the solution slide around the outcome: e.g., \"Indie SaaS founders reclaim 20+ hours/week — one CLI replaces four tool integrations.\" Then show the mechanism (wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, Vercel) as the supporting detail, not the headline."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_dodged_competition",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no competition slide. Across all 6 slides, there is no mention of alternatives, incumbent tools, or differentiators. DocSend data shows investors scrutinize the competition section more than founders expect, and VCs spend 88% more time on competition in funded decks. Absence of a competition slide reads as either unawareness of the competitive landscape or unwillingness to address it — both are disqualifying signals.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide. At minimum cover: direct competitors (other multi-tool CLI or workflow-automation products for SaaS builders), each named platform's native offering (GitHub Actions, Stripe CLI, Vercel integrations), and the status quo (manual duct-tape). For each, state 2–3 concrete differentiators."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_retention_rebased",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no retention data of any kind. Slide 4 states $8K MRR and 18% MoM growth but no churn rate, no retention cohort, and no Month 3 rebasing. The a16z standard for AI companies requires retention analysis rebased from Month 3 to filter out novelty users. This is entirely absent.",
      "notes": "Add a retention metric to the traction slide: at minimum, a monthly churn rate or a 3-month retention rate. For an \"AI-native\" product, rebasing from Month 3 (filtering the initial curiosity cohort) is the a16z-standard presentation that demonstrates durable engagement."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_cost_to_serve_shown",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck makes no mention of compute costs, COGS, gross margin, or inference economics anywhere in its 6 slides. The a16z standard for AI companies requires showing the COGS breakdown (inference, data, hosting) and a path to 60%+ gross margins. The \"AI-native\" claim amplifies investor concern about margin structure, making the absence more significant than it would be for a non-AI product.",
      "notes": "Add a gross margin figure or estimate to the business model slide (once added). Even a single line — e.g., \"Current gross margin: ~75% (no significant inference costs at current scale)\" — addresses the investor question the \"AI-native\" label raises."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_defensibility_beyond_model",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck's only AI-related content is the \"AI-native\" label on slide 1. There is no description of proprietary data, feedback loops, workflow embeddedness, vertical specialization, agentic capabilities, or any other defensibility layer. The a16z AI-company framework treats \"we use [foundation model]\" as an insufficient moat; the deck does not even reach that level of specificity — it simply claims \"AI-native\" with no elaboration.",
      "notes": "On the solution slide or a dedicated \"Why Us\" slide, state what accumulates over time that a competitor cannot replicate: e.g., workflow data across GitHub/Linear/Stripe/Vercel integrations that improves routing decisions, or deep embeddedness in the founder's deploy pipeline that creates high switching costs."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_responsible_controls",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck contains no mention of privacy, data handling, eval and monitoring, guardrails, or compliance roadmap (SOC2, HIPAA, etc.) across any of its 6 slides. The a16z responsible-AI standard requires addressing these for an enterprise-facing AI product, particularly one that wraps four tools that handle code (GitHub), project data (Linear), financial data (Stripe), and infrastructure (Vercel). The absence is notable given the sensitivity of the data the product would touch.",
      "notes": "Add one slide or a brief section on the ask slide addressing: what data Acmecorp stores vs. passes through, whether SOC2 is on the roadmap, and any guardrails on CLI command execution. For a product handling Stripe data and code deploys, enterprise buyers will ask about this before signing."
    },
    {
      "id": "numbers_consistent",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The figures stated across all 6 slides are: $8K MRR (slide 4), 24 paying customers (slide 4), 18% MoM growth over the last 4 months (slide 4), $1.2M raise (slide 6), $8M post-money valuation (slide 6). These figures are mutually consistent — no contradictions between slides, no ARR figure that conflicts with the MRR, and no customer count on one slide that differs from another. The reconciliation artifact for this run confirmed no arithmetic contradictions in the stated figures."
    },
    {
      "id": "data_room_ready",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck makes no mention of a cap table, historical P&L, burn rate, or data room across any of its 6 slides. The a16z data room guide treats cap table, historical P&L, and burn as baseline diligence items that should be referenced or implied available. The ask slide (slide 6) states the raise amount and valuation but provides no indication that supporting materials exist.",
      "notes": "Add one line to the ask slide indicating diligence readiness: e.g., \"Data room available: cap table, P&L, customer contracts.\" This signals operational maturity and removes a potential source of friction for investors who want to move quickly."
    },
    {
      "id": "contact_info_present",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No email address, LinkedIn URL, or other contact information appears anywhere in the deck across all 6 slides. The pre-send checklist baseline requires contact information clearly visible, typically on the cover or closing slide. An investor who finishes this deck and wants to reach out has no immediate path to do so.",
      "notes": "Add the founding team's email addresses and LinkedIn URLs to either the title slide (slide 1) or the ask slide (slide 6). The ask slide is the natural closing point and the moment an interested investor will look for next steps."
    }
  ]
}
