{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "purpose_clear",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The title slide reads \"Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders.\" This names a category (developer tools) and an ICP (indie SaaS founders) but states no quantified outcome. Sequoia's framework requires a single declarative sentence in the form \"[Category] for [ICP] that delivers [quantified outcome]\" — the outcome half is missing, so the purpose cannot be repeated by an investor after 10 seconds without adding their own interpretation.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the title-slide headline as a single declarative sentence that names the outcome: e.g., \"Acmecorp eliminates the 20+ hours/week indie SaaS founders spend on tooling integration — one CLI, zero glue code.\""
    },
    {
      "id": "headlines_carry_story",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Two slides carry conclusion headlines: slide 2 (\"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration\") and slide 4 (\"$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth\"). The remaining four read as topic labels: slide 1 is a tagline, slide 3 (\"Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI\") is a feature description, slide 5 (\"2 co-founders, ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product)\") is a roster entry, and slide 6 (\"Raising $1.2M seed at $8M post\") states a fact rather than a conclusion. DocSend and Sequoia both hold that investors skim headlines first; each headline should be a conclusion, not a topic.",
      "notes": "Rewrite slide 3's headline to an outcome: e.g., \"One command replaces 4 hours of manual integration.\" Rewrite slide 5 to founder-market fit: e.g., \"The team that built Stripe's CLI and Notion's API now owns the SaaS integration stack.\""
    },
    {
      "id": "narrative_arc_present",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The six slides follow a recognisable skeleton: Problem (slide 2) → Solution (slide 3) → Traction (slide 4) → Team (slide 5) → Ask (slide 6). The arc exists. However, six slides required by the seed framework are absent — Why Now, Market, Competition, Business Model, GTM, and Financials — leaving major gaps. Sequoia, DocSend, and YC all treat these as required; their absence breaks the arc's logical inevitability.",
      "notes": "Add the six missing slides in the correct positions: Why Now after Problem, Market after Solution, Competition after Market, Business Model after Competition, GTM after Business Model, and Financials before Ask. Each should flow from the one before it."
    },
    {
      "id": "strongest_proof_early",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The traction slide ($8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth over the last 4 months) appears on slide 4. DocSend data shows that 82% of readers who reach slide 4 finish the deck, and the strongest proof appearing by slide 4 satisfies this criterion. The traction slide is the strongest evidence point in the deck and is correctly positioned."
    },
    {
      "id": "story_stands_alone",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no market slide, no competition slide, no business model slide, no GTM slide, and no financials slide. Every investor looking at this deck without a founder present would be left asking: How big is the opportunity? Who are the competitors? How does revenue scale? What does the $1.2M buy? What is the gross margin? DocSend's share-deck standard requires the deck to be self-explanatory within ~2:30 of real attention; six unanswered structural questions mean the deck cannot stand alone.",
      "notes": "Before sending this deck to any investor, add the six missing slides (Why Now, Market, Competition, Business Model, GTM, Financials) so all standard investor questions are answered within the deck itself."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem_quantified",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 2 states \"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration\" — this quantifies the pain in time and names the ICP. However, the Sequoia problem-slide framework also requires a dollar cost of inaction, a budget owner, and ideally a customer quote or concrete example. None of those are present. A time cost alone does not tell an investor what the founder's tooling integration is worth, or whether there is a real budget to capture.",
      "notes": "Translate the 20+ hours/week to a dollar figure (e.g., at a $100/hr effective rate for a solo SaaS founder, that is $100K+ per year in opportunity cost) and add one customer quote from a real user. This gives the problem a price tag and makes it feel real rather than asserted."
    },
    {
      "id": "solution_shows_workflow",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 3 reads: \"Acmecorp wraps GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel into one CLI.\" This is a feature description (what the product does) rather than a workflow transformation (what changes for the user). There is no before/after illustration, no product screenshot, no demo link, and no statement of the value delivered. YC advises showing the workflow change; DocSend data shows seed investors now expect at minimum a prototype or workflow evidence.",
      "notes": "Replace the single sentence with a 3-step before/after: \"Before: wire webhook keys, configure deploys, reconcile billing — 4 hours. After: `acme deploy` — done in 2 minutes.\" Add a product screenshot or a Loom link to make the product feel real."
    },
    {
      "id": "why_now_has_catalyst",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no Why Now slide. Sequoia's framework explicitly asks \"Why wasn't this built before?\" — the absence of a catalyst slide means the problem reads as chronic-but-tolerable rather than urgent. The deck text across all 6 slides contains no mention of a macro shift, regulatory change, new platform primitive, or behavioral change that makes this the right moment.",
      "notes": "Add a Why Now slide after Problem. Candidate catalysts: GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel each launched major API and platform primitives in 2022–2024 that made this integration layer newly possible; the indie SaaS market has grown 5x since 2020; LLM-based tooling now makes CLI orchestration dramatically cheaper to build. Pick the strongest one and state it in one sentence."
    },
    {
      "id": "market_bottom_up",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no market slide of any kind. Sequoia and YC both flag the absence of market sizing as a critical gap — investors cannot calibrate whether the opportunity justifies a venture return without a TAM/SAM/SOM estimate. There is no bottom-up or top-down sizing anywhere in the 6 slides.",
      "notes": "Add a bottom-up market slide: count indie SaaS founders globally (available from Indie Hackers, Stripe, Product Hunt data), multiply by average tooling spend per founder per year, and show addressable share. Then layer in a second market (mid-market SaaS teams, agencies) as expansion potential. Bottom-up beats a generic TAM chart with investors."
    },
    {
      "id": "competition_honest",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no competition slide. DocSend data shows VCs spent 88% more time on the competition section in funded decks than in unfunded ones. The deck text across all 6 slides contains no mention of alternatives, incumbent tools, or differentiators. \"No competition slide\" is treated as a red flag equivalent to \"we have no competitors\" by most seed investors.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide mapping: (1) direct competitors (other CLI or workflow-automation tools for SaaS builders), (2) each named platform's own native solution (GitHub Actions, Stripe CLI, Vercel integrations), and (3) the \"do nothing\" / manual duct-tape status quo. For each, name 2–3 concrete differentiators — speed, switching costs, data advantage, or distribution."
    },
    {
      "id": "business_model_clear",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no business model slide. The 6 slides contain no mention of pricing structure, who pays vs. who uses the product, or gross margin. Sequoia's framework requires pricing model (seat, usage, outcome, per-workflow, or take-rate), buyer vs. user distinction, and gross margin structure — especially critical given the \"AI-native\" claim, which triggers investor questions about inference costs.",
      "notes": "Add a business model slide stating: (1) pricing model (e.g., $X/month per seat or per integration call), (2) who the buyer is (the indie SaaS founder themselves), and (3) gross margin estimate. Given the \"AI-native\" positioning, address compute cost and margin trajectory even if briefly."
    },
    {
      "id": "gtm_has_proof",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no GTM slide. Sequoia and DocSend both require ICP definition, channel (PLG, sales-led, or partner-led), and early channel proof (conversion rates, pipeline, or CAC) on a dedicated GTM slide. The traction slide states $8K MRR and 18% MoM growth but never explains how those customers were acquired or whether that acquisition method is repeatable.",
      "notes": "Add a GTM slide explaining: how current customers were acquired (inbound via GitHub/Product Hunt, community-led, or referral), the ICP defined precisely (indie SaaS founders at what revenue stage?), and one early channel metric (conversion rate, referral %, or CAC). If growth is entirely organic word-of-mouth, say so — that is a PLG signal, not a weakness."
    },
    {
      "id": "team_has_depth",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 5 reads: \"2 co-founders, ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product).\" No names are given, so an investor cannot look up or verify the credentials. No specific roles, tenures, or accomplishments at Stripe or Notion are stated. No photos, no LinkedIn links, no advisory board. DocSend data shows investors spend ~46 seconds (~30% of total deck attention) on the team slide — a single-sentence roster wastes that attention budget.",
      "notes": "Add founder names, headshots, and LinkedIn URLs. For each founder, add one specific accomplishment relevant to Acmecorp: e.g., \"Led Stripe's CLI infrastructure, used by 2M+ developers\" or \"Built Notion's API platform from 0 to GA.\" Add one line on how long the co-founders have worked together. Consider listing one credible advisor in developer tools or B2B SaaS."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_structure",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The detected stage is seed (high confidence). The seed framework expects ~10 slides covering Title, Problem, Solution, Why Now, Market, Traction, Competition, Business Model, Team, and Ask. The deck has 6 slides and is missing Why Now, Market, Competition, Business Model, GTM, and Financials — six of the ten required seed slides. The structure that exists follows a reasonable order, but the missing slides are not optional at seed.",
      "notes": "Expand the deck to at least 10 slides by adding the six missing sections. Prioritise Market and Competition first, as investors treat their absence as the most immediate disqualifier at seed stage."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_traction",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Slide 4 states \"$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth (last 4 months).\" MRR and month-over-month growth are present, which are two of the core seed-stage KPIs. However, churn/retention, NRR, and CAC — which DocSend and a16z flag as required seed KPIs alongside MRR and growth — are entirely absent. Retention is the most predictable investor question after seeing growth: 18% MoM without a retention figure leaves the question open.",
      "notes": "Add at least one retention metric to the traction slide: monthly churn rate or net revenue retention. Even a single data point such as \"<5% monthly churn\" or \"net retention >100%\" closes the most predictable follow-up question. Add an MRR growth chart to make the trajectory visual."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_financials",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has no financials slide. For seed stage, a16z and Carta expect an operating model with revenue assumptions, gross margin, and an 18–24 month runway projection. The ask slide (slide 6) states the raise amount ($1.2M at $8M post) but provides no burn rate, no runway calculation, and no revenue milestone the raise is sized to reach.",
      "notes": "Add a simple financials slide showing: current monthly burn, current gross margin (or estimate), and a 18–24 month projection showing how the $1.2M raise maps to the next revenue milestone and runway. This also supports the ask slide by giving it a logical foundation."
    },
    {
      "id": "ask_ties_to_milestones",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "Slide 6 reads: \"Raising $1.2M seed at $8M post.\" There is no use-of-funds breakdown, no milestones tied to the dollars, no runway figure, and no next-round readiness plan. The 2026 standard (Sequoia, YC, Carta) is explicit: the ask must tie dollars → milestones → next-round readiness or a default-alive path. \"We're raising $1.2M\" is the opening clause of that sentence, not the whole thing.",
      "notes": "Expand the ask slide to include: a 3-line use-of-funds breakdown (e.g., \"2 engineers — $600K; runway to $50K MRR — $400K; infrastructure and GTM — $200K\"), the milestone this round funds (e.g., \"Reach $50K MRR in 18 months\"), and what that milestone sets up (e.g., \"Series A-ready\"). Add a runway figure in months."
    },
    {
      "id": "round_size_realistic",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The deck asks for $1.2M at $8M post. PitchBook/NVCA and Carta 2025/2026 data place the seed range at $2M–$6M. $1.2M sits below the typical seed floor and closer to the pre-seed ceiling ($250K–$2.5M). The ask is not dramatically out of range, but it is below the current seed median (~$3.8M per PitchBook/NVCA) and may signal under-ambition or an underestimated hiring plan.",
      "notes": "Either address the sub-median round size proactively in the ask slide (e.g., \"Efficient deployment: $1.2M funds 18 months to $50K MRR at current burn\") or reconsider whether $1.2M is sufficient to reach a Series A-ready milestone. Investors will ask about this gap."
    },
    {
      "id": "one_idea_per_slide",
      "status": "pass",
      "verified_by": "inferred",
      "evidence": "Based on the text content, each of the 6 slides covers a single concept: title/purpose (slide 1), problem (slide 2), solution (slide 3), traction (slide 4), team (slide 5), ask (slide 6). Word counts range from 12–18 words per slide, which is consistent with single-idea slides. No visual evidence was available (all slides have visual_evidence_captured: false; input_format is text), so this is inferred from content structure."
    },
    {
      "id": "minimal_text",
      "status": "pass",
      "verified_by": "inferred",
      "evidence": "Word count estimates across all 6 slides range from 12 to 18 words per slide, which is well within the minimal-text standard (no dense paragraphs, big type). No visual rendering was available to confirm font size or layout (all slides have visual_evidence_captured: false; input_format is text), so this is inferred from word count data."
    },
    {
      "id": "slide_count_appropriate",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has 6 core slides. The pass threshold is 10–12 slides; the fail threshold is 6 or fewer. At 6 slides, the deck is at the fail boundary. Storydoc data shows ~10-slide decks achieve the highest completion rate (32% vs. 22% average), and the seed framework requires at least 10 slides to cover the required sections.",
      "notes": "Expand the deck to 10–12 slides by adding the six missing sections (Why Now, Market, Competition, Business Model, GTM, Financials). Do not add filler slides — each addition should carry a substantive answer to a predictable investor question."
    },
    {
      "id": "consistent_design",
      "status": "warn",
      "verified_by": "not_possible",
      "evidence": "All slides have visual_evidence_captured: false and input_format is text, so no rendered pages were available for assessment. Design consistency (color palette, fonts, spacing) cannot be observed from text content alone. This criterion cannot be meaningfully evaluated without visual access to the slides.",
      "notes": "When preparing the visual deck, ensure 2–3 primary colors, 1–2 fonts, and generous white space throughout. Test consistency by viewing all slides side-by-side before sending."
    },
    {
      "id": "mobile_readable",
      "status": "pass",
      "verified_by": "inferred",
      "evidence": "Word counts across all 6 slides are 12–18 words per slide, suggesting minimal text that would likely pass the phone test. No visual rendering was available to confirm font size (all slides have visual_evidence_captured: false; input_format is text). Inferred from low word density: sparse text slides are generally mobile-readable."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_vague_purpose",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The title slide uses \"AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders.\" The ICP (\"indie SaaS founders\") and category (\"developer tools\") are specific. However, \"AI-native\" is a buzzword the deck never substantiates — the inventory records no ML in the value prop, no inference/training in COGS, and no foundation-model mention. \"AI-native\" without corroborating evidence inside the deck reads as positioning hype, which is one of the common-mistake patterns Sequoia and a16z flag.",
      "notes": "Either substantiate \"AI-native\" with a concrete one-liner on what the AI component does (e.g., \"AI-powered intent routing across GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel\"), or remove the label until a later slide can support it. Do not open with a claim the rest of the deck does not back up."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_nice_to_have_problem",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "\"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration.\" Twenty-plus hours per week is a significant recurring cost, which suggests real urgency. However, the problem slide does not identify a budget owner, does not state a dollar cost of inaction, and includes no customer voice. Without those, the problem could be read as a chronic annoyance the founder tolerates rather than a burning issue they will pay to solve. Common-mistake guidance requires showing urgency, a budget owner, and cost of inaction.",
      "notes": "Add a dollar cost of inaction (20+ hours/week at a typical SaaS founder's opportunity cost = $X/year) and one customer quote attributing the pain to a specific real situation. This converts the problem from asserted to felt."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_hype_without_proof",
